<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Peering</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>Peering</string>
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>pl.dmq.peering</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>peering</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSBonjourServices</key>
	<array>
		<string>_remotedesktop._tcp</string>
	</array>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Peering uses the camera to scan the one-time pairing code shown on your Mac.</string>
	<key>NSFaceIDUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Peering uses Face ID to protect access to your paired Macs.</string>
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Peering connects to your Mac on the local network to stream its screen and send input.</string>
	<key>NSSupportsLiveActivities</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	  Export compliance, answered once and attached to every build from here on.

	  Without this key App Store Connect asks the same encryption question on every single
	  upload, and the answer does not change between them.

	  `false` is the declaration that the app uses only encryption that is exempt from the
	  documentation requirements, and that is the case here on both counts that matter. Every
	  algorithm is a standard one: P-256 key agreement, HKDF-SHA256, ChaCha20-Poly1305,
	  SHA-256, and TLS to the relay. That rules out the "proprietary or non-standard" limb.
	  And none of it is implemented here: the app imports `Crypto`, which on an Apple platform
	  compiles its own implementation away and re-exports CryptoKit, so the work is done by
	  corecrypto in the operating system. There is no third-party crypto library in the bundle.
	  See swift-crypto's own README, which says exactly that about Apple platforms.

	  What this is not is a decision this file can make for you. It is a declaration to Apple
	  and, through them, to the US government, and the app has to stay inside it: a proprietary
	  cipher, or a vendored crypto library, would make it false and would need a classification
	  instead. The Mac host carries the same reasoning in its own plist.
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<!--
	  What keeps a session alive once the app is not on screen. iOS suspends an app that has
	  nothing to do, and a suspended app's sockets die, which is what ended every session the
	  moment the phone was locked or another app was opened, and what made the Live Activity
	  only ever live for the thirty seconds of grace.

	  `audio` is the honest-ish one: the app plays silence for exactly as long as there is a
	  session to keep. It is also what Picture in Picture needs in order to start on its own
	  when the app goes to the background.

	  This is a real cost. It keeps the radio and the CPU awake for the whole time the app is
	  backgrounded with a session up, and it is the specific thing Apple rejects apps for.
	  Both are fine for a personal, sideloaded tool and neither would be for anything shipped.

	  Note this lives here and not in an INFOPLIST_KEY_ build setting: that mechanism silently
	  ignores array-valued keys, `UIBackgroundModes` among them. It compiles, it builds, and
	  nothing appears in the plist.
	-->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>audio</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
